  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIncreasing southwesterly flow out ahead of an approaching shortwave \nwill once again result in showers and storms with heavy downpours \ndeveloping across the region. PW values approaching 2 inches, along \nwith light steering flow will result in a renewed threat for flash \nflooding to the area. There is even the possibility of an isolated \nstrong to severe storm with a damaging wind threat provided storms \ncan become organized. A flood watch has been issued for much of the \nforecast area later today and late tonight, when storm activity \nshould decrease with departure of the wave and loss of heating. \nGreatest risk area looks to be the Mid Ohio Valley region and \npossibly into north central WV areas where soils are more vulnerable \ndue to recent rains, and where latest runs of the HREF/REFS have a \ngreater than 50% chance of rainfall exceeding 3 inches. Did elect to \ngo ahead and add a tier of counties east to the watch, although not \nas much confidence exists in much in the way of flooding for these \nareas, but took into account the terrain and potential for heavy \ndownpours with any storms causing issues. \n\nConditions overall look to be drier on Sunday, although a few \npassing weak waves may generate some showers earlier in the \nday. A front will move into the area Sunday evening/night with \nadditional showers/storms possible, with an isolated threat of \nsevere weather providing storms can become organized and survive\ninto our area. With timing of the front arrival later in the \nday for much of our area, not expecting much of a threat for our\nregion.\n\nThe weather will turn increasingly warm as we progress into next \nweek as an upper ridge takes hold across the area. Differences exist \ntowards the end of the week in the models concerning timing of \nbreakdown of ridge and return of unsettled conditions.",
